Purpose & context Mathieu is an electrical engineering student at ÉTS (École de Technologie Supérieure) in Montréal building a household management PWA called APP-M&M (app-menage). The project serves a dual purpose: acquiring transferable web development skills relevant to employment and internships, and delivering a genuinely useful app for his household (mixed iOS/Android environment). The three core modules are shared schedules (with Google Calendar sync), shared to-do lists, and a recipe section. A running coach feature and food/nutrition database integration are also planned for later phases. Primary learning resource: The Odin Project (TOP) Foundations path, followed by a planned pivot to javascript.info, react.dev, and Supabase docs. Academic success (including an upcoming systems programming course, ELE216, conducted in C on GNU/Linux) takes explicit priority over the personal project. Target stack: React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind, Supabase (BaaS). App targets mobile format (max-width ~400px). GitHub: recursive-embedded-DEV | Repo: app-menage --- Current state Mathieu has completed TOP Foundations Phase 1 through CSS fundamentals and built a fully deployed static HTML/CSS mockup of the to-do list screen — published on GitHub Pages. The mockup includes a fixed bottom nav bar, a header with back navigation, checkable tasks with CSS strikethrough (input:checked + span), and an add-task section. It was explicitly scoped as a throwaway prototype (no JavaScript, to be rebuilt in React later). Sessions are numbered sequentially and documented in a structured journal. The most recent sessions covered HTML boilerplate, semantic HTML, lists, links, anchors, GitHub Pages deployment, and CSS styling patterns. CSS corrections in the latest session included: proper <a> vs <button> semantics (navigate vs act), removing align-items on non-flex elements, and avoiding empty interactive elements. A phased roadmap exists with Phase 3 (working shared to-do list with Supabase) identified as the key early milestone. --- On the horizon Continuing TOP Foundations (JavaScript section upcoming) Rebuilding the to-do list screen in React once JS foundations are in place Dual-boot Ubuntu installation (deferred to a later date, needed for ELE216 lab requirements involving hardware profiling and FPGA work) Validating RACE session warmup/cooldown values in the running plan data against original PDFs before regenerating plans.json Post-Phase-3 backlog: meal planner, real-time shared shopping list, household dashboard with charts, chore rotation engine, shared expense splitting — all explicitly deferred per anti-scope-creep principle --- Key learnings & principles "Construis, ne collectionne pas" — Mathieu's personal principle against passive resource accumulation; applied to cheatsheets, tools, and features alike Anti-scope-creep discipline — features and tools are explicitly deferred until the appropriate phase Understand the "why" before executing — Mathieu consistently wants rationale before running commands or applying techniques Atomic commits and the Git three-zone model (working directory → staging → repository) are internalized from early sessions PWA over native iOS — ruled out Swift/SwiftUI in favor of transferable web skills given household's mixed device environment GPS background tracking not achievable on iOS PWA — screen-lock kills position updates; accepted workaround is foreground-only GPS or Strava integration Data modeling principles from the running plan work: store durations in seconds as integers, use zero-convention (zero = block doesn't exist), separate plan templates from user instances, keep derived data calculated not stored --- Approach & patterns Session-based, documented learning: each session is numbered, timed, and logged in a structured journal (date, phase, concepts, blockers, progress, next steps, resources) During active study sessions: Mathieu shares notes progressively and prefers Claude to listen and synthesize at the end rather than interrupt with explanations Code review loop: Mathieu submits code → receives targeted feedback → corrects himself → resubmits; corrections are precise, not prescriptive Honest curation over exhaustive lists: Mathieu values filtered recommendations with clear reasoning for exclusions (e.g., why certain tutorial sites were deprioritized) Direct communication: flags inconsistencies immediately, pushes back on pedagogical tangents when the goal is a concrete deliverable Communicates with Claude in French --- Tools & resources Development: VS Code (with GitHub settings sync), Git Bash (for Unix commands on Windows), Node.js LTS, npm, Vite Version control: Git + GitHub (app-menage repo), GitHub Pages for live deploys References: MDN (primary CSS/HTML reference), devhints.io (live coding companion), quickref.me Data: Excel workbook for running plan data (plans, seances, types, efforts, readme sheets); compiled to plans.json via compile-plans.js Documentation: Local documentation/ folder (Word journal + roadmap files) — excluded from .gitignore, not version-controlled (migration to versioned Markdown flagged as a pending decision) Environment note: Currently on Windows; dual-boot Ubuntu deferred but planned for ELE216 requirements

